WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.133 --> 00:00:00.850
Hey everyone, I'm Guy.

00:00:00.850 --> 00:00:02.018
A lot of fitness beginners have been asking—

00:00:02.018 --> 00:00:04.721
They don’t know how to plan their workouts at the gym.

00:00:04.721 --> 00:00:05.422
And they ask me—

00:00:05.422 --> 00:00:07.524
If you're training your whole body using machines only,

00:00:07.524 --> 00:00:09.476
how should you structure your workout routine?

00:00:09.476 --> 00:00:10.827
So today I’ve put together

00:00:10.827 --> 00:00:12.529
a full-body machine workout split across 4 days.

00:00:12.529 --> 00:00:13.913
Just 13 exercises in total.

00:00:13.913 --> 00:00:14.898
Super simple.

00:00:14.898 --> 00:00:15.648
Very forgiving.

00:00:15.648 --> 00:00:17.250
It’ll definitely make you fall in love with training.

00:00:17.934 --> 00:00:19.135
These 13 exercises

00:00:19.135 --> 00:00:20.320
are just a general framework.

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:21.771
As you progress over time,

00:00:21.771 --> 00:00:23.006
you’ll gradually increase the weight.

00:00:23.006 --> 00:00:24.524
Just focus on mastering these 13 moves,

00:00:24.524 --> 00:00:25.959
and you’ll see major improvements.

00:00:26.159 --> 00:00:27.327
The four days are split into:

00:00:27.327 --> 00:00:28.061
Chest,

00:00:28.061 --> 00:00:28.845
Legs,

00:00:28.845 --> 00:00:29.679
Shoulders,

00:00:29.679 --> 00:00:30.447
Back.

00:00:30.447 --> 00:00:31.281
Chest day:

00:00:31.281 --> 00:00:34.501
Chest press, chest fly, reverse fly, bicep curl.

00:00:34.501 --> 00:00:35.435
Leg day:

00:00:35.435 --> 00:00:38.221
Leg press, leg curl, knee extension.

00:00:38.221 --> 00:00:39.105
Shoulder day:

00:00:39.105 --> 00:00:42.042
Shoulder press, lateral raise, tricep pushdown.

00:00:42.042 --> 00:00:43.026
Back day:

00:00:43.026 --> 00:00:46.346
Lat pulldown (overhand), lat pulldown (underhand), seated row.

00:00:46.346 --> 00:00:48.415
You can screenshot this chart first,

00:00:48.565 --> 00:00:50.984
and record your training weights on it each time.

00:00:50.984 --> 00:00:54.020
Track whether your training intensity is improving.

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:55.522
In the full demo video,

00:00:55.522 --> 00:00:57.540
I’ll show you my current physique,

00:00:57.540 --> 00:00:59.893
and the actual weights and tempo I use when training.

00:00:59.893 --> 00:01:01.394
Just for your reference.

00:01:01.544 --> 00:01:02.462
First exercise:

00:01:02.462 --> 00:01:03.213
Chest press.

00:01:03.213 --> 00:01:04.330
Here’s the idea—

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:06.566
Weight and muscle mass are closely related.

00:01:06.566 --> 00:01:07.867
So for each training day,

00:01:07.867 --> 00:01:09.352
we always start with

00:01:09.352 --> 00:01:11.771
a heavier, higher-intensity movement.

00:01:11.771 --> 00:01:14.407
For chest press, you’re basically choosing between two machines:

00:01:14.407 --> 00:01:16.726
Seated chest press or Smith bench press.

00:01:16.726 --> 00:01:18.678
Today I’m demonstrating the seated chest press.

00:01:18.678 --> 00:01:19.596
The benefit is—

00:01:19.596 --> 00:01:20.480
It’s easy to use.

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:21.481
Very forgiving.

00:01:21.481 --> 00:01:22.565
When you sit down,

00:01:22.565 --> 00:01:23.716
don’t rush to start pushing.

00:01:23.716 --> 00:01:26.119
First, adjust the seat height properly.

00:01:26.436 --> 00:01:28.204
Then adjust the range of motion.

00:01:29.322 --> 00:01:31.574
Start with a light weight to warm up.

00:01:35.695 --> 00:01:37.097
During the warm-up,

00:01:37.097 --> 00:01:38.498
check your own posture.

00:01:39.099 --> 00:01:41.067
Is the seat too low, causing your shoulders to shrug?

00:01:41.067 --> 00:01:43.603
Are your elbows flaring out too much, causing shoulder impingement?

00:01:43.603 --> 00:01:47.140
When doing the seated chest press, you don’t need to retract your shoulder blades.

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:48.758
Just keep your shoulders down and relaxed.

00:01:48.758 --> 00:01:50.293
I only retract and depress my shoulder blades,

00:01:50.293 --> 00:01:53.029
and arch my back slightly when I’m training for powerlifting.

00:01:53.029 --> 00:01:56.182
That way, more back muscles can help support heavier loads.

00:01:56.466 --> 00:01:58.101
But when I’m training for hypertrophy,

00:01:58.101 --> 00:01:59.652
I just keep my shoulders relaxed and down.

00:01:59.853 --> 00:02:01.688
Focus on isolating the target muscles.

00:02:01.888 --> 00:02:03.490
For this exercise, my working sets are

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:05.358
72.5 kg, 12 reps, 3 sets.

00:02:05.358 --> 00:02:06.860
Rest 90 seconds between sets.

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:08.978
Watch my training tempo—

00:02:08.978 --> 00:02:11.464
Slow press, pause, slow release.

00:02:11.464 --> 00:02:12.832
And I don’t go all the way down.

00:02:13.066 --> 00:02:13.950
If you go all the way down,

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:16.486
it means you have to restart the movement each rep.

00:02:16.619 --> 00:02:17.654
It’s not that it’s wrong—

00:02:17.654 --> 00:02:19.022
But today’s goal is

00:02:19.022 --> 00:02:22.091
to focus on muscle activation and physique sculpting.

00:02:22.625 --> 00:02:24.377
Second exercise: pec deck fly.

00:02:24.377 --> 00:02:25.145
Same idea—

00:02:25.145 --> 00:02:27.297
Super simple and very forgiving.

00:02:27.297 --> 00:02:28.665
Again, after sitting down,

00:02:28.665 --> 00:02:29.833
don’t rush to start.

00:02:29.833 --> 00:02:31.651
First, adjust the seat height.

00:02:32.535 --> 00:02:34.154
Then adjust the handles.

00:02:36.105 --> 00:02:38.741
Start with a moderate weight to warm up.

00:02:46.299 --> 00:02:48.067
Every exercise after this is the same—

00:02:48.067 --> 00:02:50.353
Adjust everything before you start training.

00:02:50.353 --> 00:02:52.071
I won’t explain it verbally each time.

00:02:52.071 --> 00:02:53.606
For this exercise, my working sets are

00:02:53.606 --> 00:02:55.608
65 kg, 12 reps, 3 sets.

00:02:55.608 --> 00:02:57.026
Rest 90 seconds between sets.

00:02:57.026 --> 00:03:00.547
Training tempo: slow squeeze, pause, slow release.

00:03:00.697 --> 00:03:02.298
The goal of this exercise is

00:03:02.298 --> 00:03:05.385
to isolate the inner portion of the pectoralis major.

00:03:05.385 --> 00:03:07.554
It helps with chest hypertrophy,

00:03:07.554 --> 00:03:10.290
and improves chest muscle contraction and mind-muscle connection.

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:13.860
Great for practicing control and feeling chest activation.

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:16.112
Third exercise: reverse fly.

00:03:16.112 --> 00:03:17.063
This targets...

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:18.715
the rear delts.

00:03:19.115 --> 00:03:20.183
You might be thinking—

00:03:20.183 --> 00:03:20.767
Huh, that’s odd.

00:03:20.767 --> 00:03:23.019
Isn’t rear delts training supposed to be on shoulder day?

00:03:23.019 --> 00:03:25.455
That’s because the day after our shoulder day is what?

00:03:25.455 --> 00:03:26.322
Back day, right?

00:03:26.322 --> 00:03:27.774
And the movements on back day—

00:03:27.774 --> 00:03:29.576
they all require rear delt involvement.

00:03:29.576 --> 00:03:31.878
To avoid affecting the quality of back day training,

00:03:31.878 --> 00:03:33.796
I place rear delt exercises

00:03:33.796 --> 00:03:35.515
on chest day instead.

00:03:35.748 --> 00:03:36.716
After pec fly,

00:03:36.716 --> 00:03:38.401
you can use the same machine

00:03:38.401 --> 00:03:39.886
to train rear delts.

00:03:40.203 --> 00:03:41.738
It’s just so convenient.

00:03:41.971 --> 00:03:43.656
Start with a light warm-up set

00:03:43.656 --> 00:03:45.642
to find rear delt activation.

00:03:45.642 --> 00:03:47.043
Key point of the movement—

00:03:47.043 --> 00:03:49.762
don’t intentionally retract your scapula.

00:03:49.762 --> 00:03:51.014
That’ll shift the working area

00:03:51.014 --> 00:03:52.849
to the mid-lower traps.

00:03:52.932 --> 00:03:54.851
Of course, that’s also a training method.

00:03:55.101 --> 00:03:56.452
But if you want the load

00:03:56.452 --> 00:03:58.104
to really hit the rear delts—

00:03:58.104 --> 00:03:58.638
then we

00:03:58.638 --> 00:04:01.007
keep the scapula stable, no intentional movement.

00:04:01.341 --> 00:04:02.825
Let’s feel it with just bodyweight.

00:04:02.825 --> 00:04:04.527
Extend both arms forward.

00:04:04.527 --> 00:04:05.812
Palms facing down.

00:04:05.812 --> 00:04:07.413
This is retraction.

00:04:07.614 --> 00:04:09.082
This is protraction.

00:04:09.966 --> 00:04:11.117
Retraction.

00:04:11.968 --> 00:04:13.169
Protraction.

00:04:13.169 --> 00:04:15.238
We stay in the protracted position.

00:04:18.558 --> 00:04:20.727
If your scapula is retracted,

00:04:20.727 --> 00:04:23.746
your arms can open past 180 degrees.

00:04:24.464 --> 00:04:26.666
But when protracted,

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:29.152
you’ll hit a block around 160.

00:04:29.585 --> 00:04:31.955
And that’s the range of motion we want to train.

00:04:32.238 --> 00:04:35.291
Feel it—let the rear delts take the load.

00:04:35.808 --> 00:04:37.794
That’s the range we’re targeting.

00:04:38.161 --> 00:04:39.579
Arms extended forward.

00:04:40.163 --> 00:04:42.715
Move your arms outward in a circular motion—not retracting.

00:04:42.715 --> 00:04:46.970
Training tempo: slow open, pause, slow release.

00:04:46.970 --> 00:04:48.538
My working sets for this movement—

00:04:48.538 --> 00:04:50.540
31.5 kg, 12 reps, 3 sets.

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:52.091
Rest 90 seconds between sets.

00:04:52.091 --> 00:04:53.693
Many people, when training this movement,

00:04:53.693 --> 00:04:55.278
focus too much on the weight,

00:04:55.278 --> 00:04:57.597
causing too much back muscle involvement—

00:04:57.880 --> 00:05:00.083
and failing to isolate the rear delts properly.

00:05:00.533 --> 00:05:02.885
Rear delts aren’t a large muscle group.

00:05:02.885 --> 00:05:04.721
We need to use appropriate weight

00:05:04.721 --> 00:05:05.972
and good movement patterns.

00:05:05.972 --> 00:05:08.491
Also, compared to other areas,

00:05:08.491 --> 00:05:10.476
rear delts are harder to feel.

00:05:10.476 --> 00:05:13.029
So it’s even more important to train them with light weight.

00:05:13.713 --> 00:05:16.249
Last exercise—bicep curl.

00:05:16.249 --> 00:05:18.685
This is a muscle group most people can activate easily.

00:05:18.685 --> 00:05:21.421
In daily life, lots of movements involve the biceps.

00:05:21.421 --> 00:05:22.455
And in the gym,

00:05:22.455 --> 00:05:24.941
the most common equipment is this curl machine.

00:05:25.358 --> 00:05:28.194
Set your arms in position, then find a comfortable setup—

00:05:28.194 --> 00:05:29.595
then start with a warm-up set

00:05:29.595 --> 00:05:30.880
before moving into working sets.

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:31.914
Training tempo—

00:05:31.914 --> 00:05:34.217
slow lift, pause, slow release.

00:05:34.217 --> 00:05:35.718
And don’t go all the way down.

00:05:35.835 --> 00:05:37.337
For this movement, my working sets are

00:05:37.337 --> 00:05:39.222
31.5 kg, 12 reps, 3 sets.

00:05:39.222 --> 00:05:40.723
Rest 90 seconds between sets.

00:05:40.873 --> 00:05:42.558
When training biceps—

00:05:42.558 --> 00:05:45.111
if you’re flying off the pad during the concentric phase,

00:05:45.111 --> 00:05:46.879
using leverage to pull the weight,

00:05:46.879 --> 00:05:48.765
then the weight is too heavy for you.

00:05:48.965 --> 00:05:51.884
Lower the weight—good movement patterns are key.

00:05:52.185 --> 00:05:53.186
When you finish training today,

00:05:53.186 --> 00:05:55.154
and you’re already looking forward to the next session—

00:05:55.154 --> 00:05:57.407
that’s a very healthy mindset.

00:05:57.407 --> 00:05:58.074
Fitness is this.

00:05:58.074 --> 00:05:59.625
Whether you’re in a good mood,

00:05:59.625 --> 00:06:01.411
or feeling down emotionally—

00:06:01.411 --> 00:06:03.346
if you can still show up and train,

00:06:03.346 --> 00:06:04.630
you’ve already succeeded.

00:06:04.630 --> 00:06:06.382
Fitness is a lifestyle habit.

00:06:06.382 --> 00:06:08.501
You don’t need to go all out every time you enter the gym,

00:06:08.501 --> 00:06:10.670
pushing yourself into days of delayed soreness

00:06:10.670 --> 00:06:12.004
just to feel like you hit your goal.

00:06:12.171 --> 00:06:14.991
Sure, after heavy, high-intensity training,

00:06:14.991 --> 00:06:16.359
you’ll feel accomplished—

00:06:16.359 --> 00:06:17.093
and happy.

00:06:17.093 --> 00:06:19.295
But ask yourself—can you train like this

00:06:19.295 --> 00:06:21.898
four days a week, for months on end?

00:06:22.098 --> 00:06:23.666
So for beginners,

00:06:23.666 --> 00:06:25.835
I actually recommend not pushing intensity too high.

00:06:25.835 --> 00:06:27.186
Instead, focus on

00:06:27.186 --> 00:06:29.105
making progress with every session.

00:06:29.105 --> 00:06:30.590
It doesn’t have to be heavier weight—

00:06:30.590 --> 00:06:31.707
your form improves,

00:06:31.707 --> 00:06:33.009
your neural recruitment gets better,

00:06:33.009 --> 00:06:34.026
your muscle activation feels stronger—

00:06:34.026 --> 00:06:35.344
all of that is progress.

00:06:35.344 --> 00:06:38.231
Finishing each workout feeling happy,

00:06:38.231 --> 00:06:40.750
and excited for the next one is what matters most.

00:06:40.983 --> 00:06:42.652
Welcome to day two of training—

00:06:42.652 --> 00:06:43.352
Leg day.

00:06:43.352 --> 00:06:45.555
A lot of people tend to skip leg day.

00:06:45.555 --> 00:06:46.322
Always thinking...

00:06:46.322 --> 00:06:48.157
Big upper body is enough.

00:06:48.157 --> 00:06:49.142
Especially guys.

00:06:49.142 --> 00:06:49.976
But leg day—

00:06:49.976 --> 00:06:52.462
is super important for quality of life and body proportions.

00:06:52.462 --> 00:06:53.679
It really matters.

00:06:53.863 --> 00:06:55.314
First movement: leg press.

00:06:55.314 --> 00:06:56.499
You can use a leg press machine,

00:06:56.499 --> 00:06:58.668
or something like a hack squat machine.

00:06:58.668 --> 00:06:59.719
The main difference is—

00:06:59.719 --> 00:07:01.070
how much core engagement is required.

00:07:01.187 --> 00:07:03.239
Personally, I highly recommend the hack squat.

00:07:03.239 --> 00:07:04.907
But today I’ll demo the leg press machine—

00:07:04.907 --> 00:07:06.742
since it’s common and easy to use.

00:07:06.742 --> 00:07:07.393
It mainly trains

00:07:07.393 --> 00:07:10.780
the quads, glutes, hamstrings, and calves.

00:07:10.980 --> 00:07:12.348
Depending on foot placement,

00:07:12.348 --> 00:07:13.633
you’ll target different areas.

00:07:14.033 --> 00:07:14.951
The higher you place your feet,

00:07:14.951 --> 00:07:17.236
the more glutes and hamstrings get involved.

00:07:17.236 --> 00:07:18.154
The lower you place them,

00:07:18.154 --> 00:07:20.840
the more load goes to your quads—front of the thighs.

00:07:20.990 --> 00:07:23.292
Wider stance activates more adductors.

00:07:23.743 --> 00:07:25.461
At the beginning, don’t overthink it.

00:07:25.461 --> 00:07:27.063
Just find a comfortable distance—

00:07:27.063 --> 00:07:28.247
not too high, not too low.

00:07:28.247 --> 00:07:29.849
At the bottom of the movement,

00:07:29.849 --> 00:07:31.851
your knees should be slightly below your toes.

00:07:31.884 --> 00:07:33.186
Feet slightly turned out.

00:07:33.553 --> 00:07:35.354
Knees follow the direction of your toes.

00:07:35.354 --> 00:07:36.756
When training on the leg press,

00:07:36.756 --> 00:07:39.008
there are two key things to watch out for.

00:07:39.008 --> 00:07:41.777
First—keep your lower back flat against the pad.

00:07:41.777 --> 00:07:44.046
If the weight is too heavy and your back arches,

00:07:44.046 --> 00:07:45.798
it can put pressure on your spine.

00:07:46.466 --> 00:07:47.867
Second—when pushing up,

00:07:47.867 --> 00:07:49.752
don’t lock your knees completely straight.

00:07:49.752 --> 00:07:51.988
Keep a slight bend to maintain muscle tension—

00:07:51.988 --> 00:07:53.739
and protect your knee joints.

00:07:53.739 --> 00:07:54.857
For this movement, I do

00:07:54.857 --> 00:07:56.993
140 kg, 12 reps, 3 sets.

00:07:56.993 --> 00:07:58.478
Rest 90 seconds between sets.

00:07:58.611 --> 00:08:01.998
Training tempo: slow press, slow release.

00:08:02.198 --> 00:08:03.282
Coordinate with breathing—

00:08:03.483 --> 00:08:05.001
exhale slowly when pushing,

00:08:05.001 --> 00:08:06.769
inhale deeply when lowering.

00:08:07.653 --> 00:08:09.405
Second movement: leg curl machine.

00:08:09.405 --> 00:08:11.190
This strengthens your hamstrings—

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:12.592
especially the biceps femoris.

00:08:12.592 --> 00:08:14.844
There are seated and prone versions.

00:08:14.961 --> 00:08:16.512
I highly recommend the prone version.

00:08:16.512 --> 00:08:18.281
It allows your body to stay

00:08:18.281 --> 00:08:19.799
relaxed and stable,

00:08:19.799 --> 00:08:21.968
so you can focus on hamstring activation.

00:08:21.968 --> 00:08:25.087
Training tempo: slow curl, slow release.

00:08:25.087 --> 00:08:27.857
When curling, let the pad touch the back of your thighs,

00:08:27.957 --> 00:08:28.958
then slowly lower.

00:08:29.675 --> 00:08:30.643
For this movement, I use

00:08:30.643 --> 00:08:32.745
42.5 kg, 12 reps, 3 sets.

00:08:32.745 --> 00:08:34.180
Rest 90 seconds between sets.

00:08:34.180 --> 00:08:37.049
Pay attention to my range and tempo—

00:08:37.049 --> 00:08:38.000
no need to rush.

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:40.720
Move slowly and steadily through the full range.

00:08:40.803 --> 00:08:41.921
If you can’t complete the range,

00:08:41.921 --> 00:08:42.905
lower the weight.

00:08:44.557 --> 00:08:46.559
Final exercise: leg extension machine.

00:08:46.692 --> 00:08:48.377
This trains the front thigh muscles.

00:08:48.544 --> 00:08:49.512
Sit down and stabilize.

00:08:49.512 --> 00:08:50.897
Adjust the machine height.

00:08:50.980 --> 00:08:53.416
Keep your hamstrings and back pressed against the pad.

00:08:53.416 --> 00:08:56.002
Extend your legs upward, then slowly lower.

00:08:56.002 --> 00:08:58.504
This movement puts more pressure on the knees,

00:08:58.504 --> 00:09:00.273
so don’t go too heavy.

00:09:00.273 --> 00:09:02.108
Focus on a stable tempo,

00:09:02.108 --> 00:09:03.893
and feel the stretch in your quads.

00:09:03.976 --> 00:09:04.894
Training tempo—

00:09:04.894 --> 00:09:07.196
slow lift, pause, slow release.

00:09:07.463 --> 00:09:08.481
For this movement, I use

00:09:08.481 --> 00:09:10.633
72.5 kg, 12 reps, 3 sets.

00:09:10.633 --> 00:09:12.201
Rest 90 seconds between sets.

00:09:12.818 --> 00:09:14.387
That wraps up two days of training.

00:09:14.387 --> 00:09:15.588
To improve results,

00:09:15.588 --> 00:09:16.956
training alone isn’t enough—

00:09:16.956 --> 00:09:18.324
recovery is just as important.

00:09:18.324 --> 00:09:21.143
Make sure you get enough protein every day.

00:09:21.143 --> 00:09:22.595
It helps your muscles recover.

00:09:22.595 --> 00:09:24.513
After each round of breakdown, rebuild—

00:09:24.513 --> 00:09:25.231
so your muscles

00:09:25.231 --> 00:09:26.332
get stronger and stronger.

00:09:26.332 --> 00:09:27.683
Getting enough protein

00:09:27.683 --> 00:09:28.601
helps with recovery,

00:09:28.601 --> 00:09:29.201
and ultimately—

00:09:29.201 --> 00:09:31.187
helps improve your physique.

00:09:31.187 --> 00:09:32.572
Phargoods is currently offering

00:09:32.572 --> 00:09:33.456
soy protein powder.

